                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the discourse`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Before you choose a single word, audit the narrative landscape. Calderwood's identity is a <strong>century-old, people-first mutual</strong> — local, human, branch-based — and its staff and members are deeply attached to that story. Existing metaphors are already shaping how Project Horizon will be heard: “family”, “fortress”, “machine”. Read them, because each <em>enables</em> some interpretations and <em>constrains</em> others. And read the hard number: only <strong>34% of staff</strong> believe leadership is honest about why change happens. That is not a comms gap — it is a credibility gap your framing must overcome before anyone will believe a word of Horizon.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the framing metaphor`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `The central decision. Pick <strong>one governing metaphor</strong> for Horizon and own its trade-offs. A “journey” enables progress and shared destination but can <em>constrain honesty about loss</em>. A “machine/efficiency” frame enables clarity on the cost-to-income target but tells 1,850 people whose identity is human service that they are cogs being optimised. Oswick's lesson: a metaphor foregrounds some meanings and silences others — so name what your choice <em>enables</em>, name what it <em>suppresses</em>, and name the framing you are deliberately rejecting. Remember 480 roles are really changing; framing that hides that loss is spin, not framing.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Craft three messages & decide on dissent`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Write three messages — to <strong>front-line staff</strong>, to <strong>members and the community</strong> (28 closures are the only branch left in town), and to the <strong>union / employee-voice body</strong>. One metaphor can hold all three, but the same words cannot: the redeployed cashier and the mortgage-holder in a closing town do not share a concern. Then make the dissent call under the three-week clock: do you <em>engage</em> the union and sceptics as co-authors (dialogic, costs time), <em>contain</em> them, or <em>route around</em> them to hit the deadline — and risk them authoring the counter-narrative for you?`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Launch and absorb the counter-narrative`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Launch day. A viral staff post reframes your slogan — “Horizon = the horizon you'll never reach” — a closing-branch town stages a protest, and the pulse survey re-runs in <strong>48 hours</strong>. Sensemaking cannot be ordered to stop. If you issue a “cease and correct” you validate the counter-story by fighting it head-on; if you <em>reframe and absorb</em> the legitimate concern, you redirect the meaning. Decide who speaks and how, and whether to protect the 31 March date (GBP 6m of savings), phase the hardest closures, or buy time — without abandoning the credibility you have left.`,

