                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_family:        `<strong>Right read of the identity.</strong> Calderwood's people-first “family” story is the dominant frame, and any restructuring has to be heard through it. That doesn't mean you must use the family metaphor — it means you must reckon with it. A framing that ignores the mutual's identity will simply bounce off.`,
                    coach_r1_fortress:      `<strong>“Fortress” is in play, but it isn't the deepest frame here.</strong> Defending the branches is a tactic; the identity underneath is the human, mutual promise. If you mistake the surface frame for the core one, your metaphor will miss what staff actually care about.`,
                    coach_r1_machine:       `<strong>The “machine” frame exists — in the finance function.</strong> But the cost-to-income number is not how 1,850 front-line people experience Calderwood. If you let the efficiency story become the dominant one, you've already conceded the ground where resistance lives.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“People will just read the facts” is the most expensive assumption a change leader makes.</strong> Oswick's whole point is that there are no neutral facts here — every number arrives wrapped in a story, and if you don't choose the frame, the existing “family” and “machine” frames will choose it for you.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the people-first identity as the dominant frame AND read 34% as a credibility gap, not a comms gap. That means you already know your framing has to be <em>backed by honestly named losses</em> to be believed — exactly the discipline the next three rounds will test.`,
                    coach_r1_comms:         `<strong>Calling it a communications gap is the comfortable mistake.</strong> “We just haven't explained it well” assumes the message is fine and the audience is slow. At 34% trust the problem isn't clarity — it's belief. Better slides won't fix a credibility gap.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Treating 34% as background noise is how the counter-narrative gets its oxygen.</strong> Low trust is precisely the condition under which a reframed slogan goes viral. Ignore the baseline now and you'll meet it again in Round 4, louder.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>You skipped the discourse map.</strong> Choosing a metaphor without mapping the ones already in play means you can't see what your framing will collide with. The “family” story is going to meet your words whether you mapped it or not.`,
                    coach_r2_renewal:       `<strong>A renewal/growth frame is the strong fit.</strong> “Pruning so the mutual can grow” lets you be honest that something is being cut <em>and</em> point to a future — it honours the people-first identity without pretending no one loses. Now make sure the honesty is real, not decorative.`,
                    coach_r2_journey:       `<strong>“The next chapter” can work — if it carries the losses with it.</strong> A journey frame enables progress and shared destination, but it tends to <em>constrain honesty about loss</em>. 480 people know they're affected; if your chapter skips their page, the metaphor reads as spin.`,
                    coach_r2_organism:      `<strong>“Evolving to survive” feels natural — too natural.</strong> An organism frame makes the change sound inevitable and removes the human agency behind 480 decisions. Staff may hear it as “this is just happening to us”, which neither builds buy-in nor takes responsibility.`,
                    coach_r2_machine:       `<strong>You launched “streamlining the engine” to a people-first mutual.</strong> Oswick's point is that a metaphor foregrounds some meanings and silences others — your framing just told 1,850 people they're cogs being optimised. Trust was already at 34%. What did “engine” enable, and what did it cost you?`,
                    coach_r2_euphemism:     `<strong>“The next chapter of our family” sounds lovely — but 480 people are losing or moving roles and they know it.</strong> When the gap between your warm framing and their lived reality opens, the metaphor reads as spin. Oswick distinguishes framing from deception. Where do you name the loss honestly?`,
                    coach_r2_rejectnone:    `<strong>Refusing to reject any framing isn't flexibility — it's avoidance.</strong> Choosing a metaphor <em>is</em> choosing what you won't say. If you keep every option open, your messages will leak contradictory frames and staff will hear the one you least intended.`,
                    coach_r3_tuned:         `<strong>One metaphor, three tuned messages — that's the discipline.</strong> The mortgage-holder in a closing town and the redeployed cashier don't share a concern, but a single coherent frame can still hold them both. You kept the story consistent and the words audience-true.`,
                    coach_r3_generic:       `<strong>You sent essentially the same announcement to staff, members and the union.</strong> A single metaphor can hold all three — but the same words can't. Which audience did your one-size message actually fail: the worried member, the redeployed cashier, or the union you needed on side?`,
                    coach_r3_reassure:      `<strong>“Nothing fundamental changes” to a town losing its only branch is a promise reality will break.</strong> Vague reassurance to the most affected audience is exactly where the credibility gap reopens. Name the closure, the support and the timeline — specifics are what earn trust.`,
                    coach_r3_engage:        `<strong>Engaging the union as co-authors costs time — and buys you the narrative.</strong> Oswick's dialogic view treats dissent as unmanaged co-authorship, not obstruction. By bringing the sceptics in now, you've made it far harder for them to author the counter-story later.`,
                    coach_r3_contain:       `<strong>Containing the union keeps control of the message — for now.</strong> Minimal consultation meets the duty but leaves the sceptics outside the tent. They'll still make meaning about Horizon; you've just chosen not to shape it with them.`,
                    coach_r3_route:         `<strong>You decided to route around the union and push the announcement out fast.</strong> Three weeks later they author the counter-narrative for you — and now “Horizon” means something you didn't choose. Was dissent obstruction, or co-authorship you left unmanaged? What did engaging them early have cost versus what this just cost you?`,
                    coach_r4_reframe:       `<strong>Reframing beats suppression.</strong> You acknowledged the staff post's real concern and folded it back into your framing instead of fighting it. Sensemaking can't be ordered to stop — but it can be redirected, and that's the durable response to a counter-narrative.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Holding the original line unchanged lets the counter-story define the gap.</strong> Repeating your slogan while staff repeat theirs is two monologues, not a response. Without absorbing the legitimate concern, the reframed version keeps spreading.`,
                    coach_r4_suppress:      `<strong>A staff post reframed your slogan and you issued a “cease and correct”.</strong> Oswick would say you just validated the counter-story by fighting it head-on. Sensemaking can't be ordered to stop. How could you have <em>absorbed</em> or <em>redirected</em> that narrative instead of banning it?`,
                    coach_r4_dialogue:      `<strong>That's the response that lands.</strong> You reframed the counter-story AND showed up in person to answer it — closing the gap between your sensegiving and their sensemaking. The pulse survey re-runs into a conversation, not a broadcast.`,
                    coach_r4_silence:       `<strong>Going quiet hands the 48 hours to the counter-narrative.</strong> Silence in a low-trust launch reads as confirmation. The story doesn't pause because you stopped talking — it just runs without your voice in it.`,
                    coach_r4_protect:       `<strong>Protecting the date saves the GBP 6m — but raises the heat.</strong> Launching hard into a live counter-narrative is defensible if your framing is strong; on weak buy-in it pours fuel on the resistance. The deadline isn't the only thing you can lose.`,
                    coach_r4_delay:         `<strong>Delaying two weeks calms the story but costs GBP 6m of savings.</strong> Sometimes that trade is right — but a delay can also read as a retreat that proves the critics had a point. Buying time only helps if you use it to reframe, not just to wait.`,

