                    insight_landed:         `Change landed: you read the people-first identity, chose a metaphor that named the loss honestly, engaged dissent as co-authorship, and reframed the counter-narrative instead of suppressing it. Buy-in cleared the credibility gap, the story stayed yours, and the deadline held.`,
                    insight_backfired:      `Framing backfired: somewhere the words got ahead of the truth, or the change leader tried to win the story by force. On a people-first mutual at 34% trust, a machine frame, a euphemism, or a “cease and correct” is all it takes to hand the narrative to your staff.`,
                    insight_short:          `You moved buy-in but didn't fully close the gap. The framing, the messages and the dissent call still pull in slightly different directions — enough for the counter-narrative to find the seam between your sensegiving and their sensemaking.`,
                    insight_machine:        `The machine/efficiency frame did exactly what Oswick warns of: it foregrounded cost and silenced humanity, telling 1,850 people whose identity is human service that they are cogs. On a people-first mutual that is the single most expensive word choice you can make.`,
                    insight_euphemism:      `A warm framing that hid the 480 lost roles opened a credibility gap the moment reality contradicted it. Framing is not deception — the metaphor only holds if it can carry the loss honestly.`,
                    insight_routed:         `Routing around the union saved a little time and cost you authorship: the sceptics wrote the counter-narrative you then had to absorb. Dialogic engagement is expensive up front and cheap in the end.`,
                    insight_suppressed:     `Issuing a “cease and correct” validated the counter-story by fighting it head-on. Sensemaking can't be ordered to stop — the durable move was to reframe and absorb the concern, not to ban it.`,
                    insight_slow:           `You protected credibility but lost rollout speed — and with it some of the GBP 6m the window was meant to bank. Buying time is sometimes right, but every week of slippage has a price the board will count.`,
                    insight_dialogic:       `You closed the sensegiving/sensemaking gap: by tuning each message, engaging dissent and reframing rather than suppressing, you led change through dialogue, not broadcast. That is what makes framing durable.`,

                    hint_machine:           `The machine/efficiency frame is fighting your people-first identity. Re-frame Horizon around renewal or journey — and name the loss honestly — before resistance hardens.`,
                    hint_suppress:          `You tried to suppress the counter-narrative — that validates it. Acknowledge the legitimate concern and reframe it into your story; sensemaking can't be ordered to stop.`,
                    hint_euphemism:         `Your framing is hiding the 480-role loss; that reads as spin. Push the honesty up and name the loss inside the metaphor, or the credibility gap reopens.`,
                    hint_routed:            `You routed around the union — expect them to author the counter-narrative. Engage dialogically now to take authorship back before launch.`,
                    hint_resist:            `Resistance is dangerously high — counter-stories are compounding. Tune your messages to each audience and reframe, don't suppress.`,
                    hint_speed:             `Rollout speed has slipped and GBP 6m of savings is at risk. Phase the hardest closures so you protect the date without inflaming the counter-narrative.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — buy-in above target and resistance low. Lock it in: answer the counter-narrative in person and reframe rather than repeat.`,

